## 3–5 Reusable Findings

1. **Six governing principles** (feeds S6): The Three Lines Model is structured around six principles — (1) Governance, (2) Governing body roles, (3) Management and first/second line roles, (4) Third line roles, (5) Third line independence, and (6) Creating and protecting value — providing a ready-made structural checklist for mapping AI governance accountability across "lines" in BFSI institutions.

2. **Replacement of rigid "defence" framing with integrated roles** (feeds S6): The 2024 update reframes the original "Three Lines of Defence" (defensive, siloed) as the "Three Lines Model" emphasising principles-based collaboration among governing body, management, and internal audit toward shared objectives — relevant to debates on whether AI risk functions should be framed as "defence" or "value enablement".

3. **Third line independence as a standalone principle** (feeds S6): Principle 5 elevates internal audit's independence from management as a distinct governance requirement (structural, reporting-line, and resourcing independence) — directly applicable to assessing whether an institution's AI-model-audit function meets independence thresholds.

4. **Defined relationships among core roles** (feeds S1): The model explicitly maps three relationship types — between management (1st/2nd line) and internal audit, between internal audit and the governing body, and among all roles collectively — offering a template for diagramming AI-governance reporting lines in practitioner decks.

